WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:05.339
Avengers: Endgame tugged on our heartstrings
plenty but a deleted scene would have straight-up

00:00:05.339 --> 00:00:06.859
yanked on them.

00:00:06.859 --> 00:00:11.070
The scene in question, recently made available
on Disney+, depicts Tony Stark visiting an

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:14.690
ethereal afterlife immediately after his universe-saving
snap.

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:18.050
There, he encounters an adult version of his
daughter Morgan.

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:22.320
While Endgame directors Joe and Anthony Russo
had previously discussed the scene, we had

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:26.550
yet to get a look at it before now and it's
every bit as poignant as you'd expect.

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:31.019
In the scene, Stark suddenly finds himself
alone in the peaceful realm, seemingly confused

00:00:31.019 --> 00:00:32.730
as to where everybody went.

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:36.510
Just as he begins to realize what must be
happening, Morgan makes her appearance.

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:40.700
At first, Stark isn't sure who he's looking
at but when Morgan offers the clue that the

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:43.870
two are "pretty close," he figures it out
pretty quickly.

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:47.870
Stark's face registers a thousand emotions
as he looks at the daughter he'll never get

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:52.030
to know, and he admits to wondering if he
made the wrong decision in sacrificing himself.

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:55.180
And, of course, the scene ends exactly as
you'd expect:

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:57.900
"I love you 3000."

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:01.920
There's amazing chemistry in the scene between
Robert Downey Jr. and Katherine Langford,

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.760
who unfortunately was robbed of an appearance
in the biggest movie of all time when this

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.210
scene hit the cutting room floor.

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:11.360
It wouldn't surprise us, though, if Morgan
Stark were to make an appearance in a future

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:12.900
MCU film, or several.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:16.650
In their commentary on the scene, the Russo
brothers explained that it was conceived as

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:19.890
a sort of bookend to a scene in Avengers:
Infinity War.

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:23.440
Specifically, the scene in which Thanos, having
just accomplished his mission to snap half

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:28.190
of all life in the universe out of existence,
was basically transported inside the Soul

00:01:28.190 --> 00:01:29.190
Stone.

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:32.240
There, he encountered a young version of his
adopted daughter Gamora, who was one of the

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:35.880
many people who died at Thanos' hands so that
he could accomplish his mission.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:36.880
Joe Russo explained:

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:41.510
"We sent him to what we would call the 'Waystation,'
to mirror what had happened to Thanos."

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:42.760
Anthony Russo added that:

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:46.970
"The idea is that this moment... that act
[of using the Infinity Stones] sort of forces

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:51.440
you to confront, or reconcile, something that's
unresolved in yourself, in your soul."

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:55.500
Joe then explained that the reason an adult
Morgan was cast was so Stark could come to

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:59.670
terms with what he had done, and truly understand
the fact that he was giving up so much time

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:02.100
spent with his daughter throughout her life.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:06.130
However, at the end of the day, the scene
was cut for reasons that are pretty understandable

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:10.560
when you're talking about a flick that already
ran in excess of three hours: pacing.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:11.560
As Joe Russo put it:

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:15.090
"When we put this in the film, we felt like
it ground the movie to a halt.

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:18.920
And [Stark] does something similar in the
next sequence, when he gives his own eulogy

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:20.599
[via hologram at his funeral]."

00:02:20.599 --> 00:02:24.700
The director continued to say that he and
his brother were mindful of adding too many

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:28.200
endings, which is a problem that popped up
in The Lord of the Rings: The Return of the

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:29.200
King.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:32.420
Audiences might have felt like the film had
ended on the perfect sentimental note, only

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:35.230
for it to continue on to another, and another.

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:36.230
According to Joe:

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:39.810
"What can happen at the end of a film this
big is you can have ending after ending after

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:42.890
ending, and it starts to feel like the movie's
never gonna end."

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:45.710
In other words, the power of the individual
moments are watered-down:

00:02:45.710 --> 00:02:52.970
"When you have too many sequences in a row,
you can dilute the value of each one of them."

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:57.110
Taking all of these things into account, the
pair reluctantly decided that the scene had

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:58.110
to go.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:00.110
Still, that doesn't mean we can't admire its
power.

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:03.151
Anyone in their right mind would admit that
there wouldn't have been a dry eye in the

00:03:03.151 --> 00:03:06.010
house at its conclusion had they left it in.

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:09.940
Unfortunately, only time will tell whether
Morgan Stark will make any further appearances

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:11.040
in the MCU.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.090
In the meantime, though, you can revisit the
triumphs and tragedies of Avengers: Endgame

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:17.670
to your heart's content with a Disney+ subscription.

